"comparison": "Both JPMorgan and Goldman Sachs disclose specific sensitivity analyses for fair value measurements under critical accounting estimates, quantifying the impact of assumption changes on financial liabilities or derivatives. JPMorgan focuses on operational assumptions (redemption rate and cost per point) impacting a loyalty liability, stating a combined increase would raise the liability by $442 million. Goldman Sachs focuses on market risk factors (credit and funding spreads) impacting derivatives and elected fair value liabilities, stating a one basis point increase would result in a $2 million loss on derivatives and a $43 million gain on liabilities. While the underlying risks differ (consumer liability modeling vs. market spread risk), both provide a specific monetary quantification of sensitivity to a defined change in assumptions as a critical accounting estimate.",
